  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nBrief upper ridging into southwesterly flow aloft between the \ndeparting trough and the next system. Models seem to be in \nsomewhat decent agreement on a lead shortwave coming up the \nsouthwest flow late Friday into Saturday, with the main upper \nlow still to the northwest over Saskatchewan and eastern MT. \nThat upper low then moves through the Northern Plains into the \nGreat Lakes on Sunday. Brief ridging between systems on Monday, \nand then another quick moving trough coming through the Dakotas \non Tuesday. Northwest to near zonal flow mid to late next week, \nwith ensemble members all over the place on various embedded \nshortwaves. \n\n...Showers Friday into Friday night...\n\nWith the lead shortwave trough and warm air advection late \nFriday into Friday night, there should be plenty of upper \nsupport for a round of light rain showers. Both CAMs and global \nmodels have the majority of the QPF up in Canada due to the \ntrack of the surface low, but could see a few hundredths up to a\ntenth of an inch of rain. The GFS even has some 100 J/kg of MU \nCAPE, so can't completely rule out a rumble of thunder in \nwestern MN although chances of that are better further south and\neast. Cold front will be pushing through Friday night, but most\nof the model soundings seem like they will be staying above the\nfreezing mark until later in the weekend. \n\n...Snow showers Saturday and Sunday...\n\nNorthwest winds and cold advection will be knocking temps down \nas we head into the weekend, with readings dropping throughout \nthe day on Saturday and well below freezing Saturday night. Most\nof the wrap around moisture on Saturday is north of the \nCanadian border, but with the main upper low approaching \nSaturday night and Sunday, could see some snow showers mainly \nalong and north of Highway 2. Probabilities of at least a tenth \nof an inch of snow are 60 percent and above for much of that \narea, although impacts will be limited due to warm ground \ntemperatures. Chances for 1 inch or more are minimal except in a\nlimited area downwind of the Turtle Mountains. At this point, \ncan't rule out seeing some flakes but at this point \nminor/advisory level impacts seem unlikely.\n\n...More seasonable temps Sunday into next week...\n\nWhile truly bitterly cold air remains out of our area, the \nupper low Sunday and additional trough passage on Tuesday will \nbring temps down to more common values for late November. ECMWF \nEFI has temps slightly negative, but not anything outside of \nclimatology. Highs should be down into the 30s or even upper \n20s, with lows in the single digits in some areas early next \nweek.",
